@meta
id: 22-02
title: Whole lines, two levels deep
chapter: 22
type: lesson
skills: visual-line
par: 34
minutes: 4
vscode: select the five lines, press Tab twice
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
`V` is visual mode for whole lines. Where the cursor sits on the line no
longer matters; `j` and `k` add lines to the selection, and the operator
you press gets every line in it, start to finish.

The five lines that do the refund in `cancel` were pasted into a new
`try` block inside a new `if`, and nobody re-indented them. They are two
levels too shallow. Indent them so the method reads properly:

    if (order.status !== 'SHIPPED') {
      try {
        await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
        await this.orderRepository.update({
          where: { id },
          values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
        });
      } catch (error) {

A count in front of `>` in visual mode is not "how many lines" — the
selection already says that. It is how many levels.

@keys
| `V` | start a line-wise selection |
| `>` `<` | shift the selection right or left by one `shiftwidth` |
| `2>` | shift it two levels in one go |

@hints
Land on the first `await` line with a search, then `V` and four `j`
to take the five lines.
--
`>` once is one level. You want two. Do not press `>` twice — put the
count in front of it.
--
`/await this.payment` `Enter`, `V4j`, `2>`.

@solution
    /await this.payment<Enter> V 4 j 2 > :wq<Enter>

The same five lines could be shifted with `5>>` from normal mode, and
then `.` for the second level. That is four keys against five, so the
visual route is not about speed here. It is about the count
meaning something different: in normal mode `4>>` counts lines, in
visual mode `2>` counts levels, because the selection has already fixed
the lines. Once you have a region selected, every count you type is
about depth or repetition, never about extent.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.status !== 'SHIPPED') {
      try {
    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
    await this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
      } catch (error) {
        throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
      }
    }

    return order;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.status !== 'SHIPPED') {
      try {
        await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
        await this.orderRepository.update({
          where: { id },
          values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
        });
      } catch (error) {
        throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
      }
    }

    return order;
  }
}
